# foundry-audit allowlist — one entry per line, `#` comments allowed.
#
# Two kinds of entry are recognised: a repo-relative path that is referenced
# deliberately before it exists, and an identifier (e.g. ADR-NNN) that appears
# in the documents as an example rather than as a reference.
#
# This file is not a place to silence findings you'd rather not fix. If an
# entry here is hiding something real, the check is right and the document is
# wrong. Every entry should be able to say why it's here.

# Foundry's docs describe foundry-audit's own dangling-reference check, which
# means writing out an example dangling ID. Foundry itself uses date-headed
# decisions, not ADR numbering, so this is subject matter, not a reference.
ADR-099

# SESSIONS.md (2026-08-17) recounts a real dangling reference this tool caught:
# a decision cited as 2026-06-30 when the entry is dated 2026-06-28. Telling
# that story requires writing the wrong date down next to the words
# "DECISIONS.md", which is exactly what the check looks for. Allowlisted rather
# than reworded, because the account is worth more than the silence — and there
# is no decision entry on this date to shadow. If one is ever written, delete
# this line first.
2026-06-30

